var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"realtime/#Real-time-loop","page":"Real-time loop","text":"run_realtime_loop! runs control_step! periodically on the calling OS thread:\n\nmlockall(MCL_CURRENT | MCL_FUTURE), CPU pinning, SCHED_FIFO;\none GC.gc(), then the GC is paused;\nclock_nanosleep(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, TIMER_ABSTIME) against absolute deadlines (no drift);\nper-cycle statistics, histograms and an optional telemetry sample;\non exit, the safe command is sent and every OS setting is restored.\n\nIf a setup step fails without strict=true, the loop warns and continues in soft real-time mode. LoopStats records which steps were actually applied.","title":"Real-time loop"},{"category":"section","location":"realtime/#Running-on-a-dedicated-thread","page":"Real-time loop","text":"spawn_realtime_loop runs the same loop on a Julia worker thread of its own (by default the last thread of the :default pool) and returns at once. The calling thread stays free for telemetry, plotting or a REPL:\n\n# julia --threads=2\nh = spawn_realtime_loop(ws, ctrl, backend, 1_000_000, typemax(Int64);\n                        telemetry=ring, priority=80, cpu=3, safe_command=[0.0])\n# ... drain `ring`, watch, tweak setpoints ...\nstop_loop!(h)              # finishes the current cycle, sends the safe command\nstats = fetch(h)           # LoopStats; abort_reason === :stopped\n\nThe task is pinned to its thread and never yields during the loop, so SCHED_FIFO and CPU affinity apply to exactly that OS thread. Any run_realtime_loop! also accepts stop=Threads.Atomic{Bool}(false) directly.","title":"Running on a dedicated thread"},{"category":"section","location":"realtime/#Checklist","page":"Real-time loop","text":"PREEMPTRT kernel; isolate a core (`isolcpus=3 nohzfull=3 rcu_nocbs=3) and passcpu=3`.\nRoot, or CAP_SYS_NICE + CAP_IPC_LOCK with ulimit -r 99 and ulimit -l unlimited. Julia reserves a lot of virtual memory, so mlockall fails under the default memlock limit.\nStart Julia with --threads=2 and keep other threads allocation-light: the GC is paused process-wide.\nWarm up with a few control_step! calls so compilation never lands on a deadline.","title":"Checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"realtime/#Measuring","page":"Real-time loop","text":"stats = run_realtime_loop!(ws, ctrl, backend, cfg)\nlatency_percentile(stats, 0.999)               # step compute time, ns\nlatency_percentile(stats, 0.999; kind=:wake)   # scheduling jitter, ns\n\nbench/compare_cyclictest.sh runs cyclictest and ArtyHill with identical period, priority and CPU, so you can see how much the Julia loop adds on top of the kernel's own latency. Tutorial: PREEMPT_RT latency walks through setting up a machine and running it.","title":"Measuring"},{"category":"section","location":"realtime/#Telemetry","page":"Real-time loop","text":"Push one TelemetrySample per cycle into an SPSCRing. It never blocks; when the ring is full, samples are dropped and counted. Drain it from another thread and save with write_telemetry_csv.","title":"Telemetry"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Safety","page":"Safety","text":"warning: Warning\nThese features reduce risk; they do not make a system safe. Always use hardware interlocks: an emergency stop that cuts power, and the drive's own limits and watchdog.","title":"Safety"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Actuator-limits","page":"Safety","text":"LimitedController wraps any controller. Non-finite commands are replaced by the previous command, each command's change per step is limited, and the result is clamped to [umin, umax].","title":"Actuator limits"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Watchdog-and-safe-command","page":"Safety","text":"cfg = RTConfig(; period_ns=1_000_000, iterations=10^6,\n               max_consecutive_overruns=5, max_consecutive_errors=3)\nstats = run_realtime_loop!(ws, ctrl, backend, cfg; safe_command=[0.0])\nstats.abort_reason      # :none, :overruns, :errors or :exception\nstats.safe_command_sent # false if even the safe command failed to reach the drive\n\nsafe_command is written on every exit: normal completion, a watchdog trip, or an exception from the controller or backend (the exception is rethrown afterwards). Call send_safe_command! yourself from an emergency-stop handler.","title":"Watchdog and safe command"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Drive-side-watchdog","page":"Safety","text":"Implement heartbeat! for your backend, or use heartbeat_id on SocketCANBackend, so the drive disables itself if the master stops. On EtherCAT, configure the slave's SyncManager watchdog.","title":"Drive-side watchdog"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ArtyHill.ArtyHill","page":"API reference","text":"ArtyHill\n\nHardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) and real-time embedded prototyping for Julia.\n\nThree decoupled layers:\n\nHardware abstraction – AbstractHILBackend with allocation-free read_sensors! / write_actuators! (pure simulation, SocketCAN, EtherCAT/SOEM).\nDeterministic runtime – zero-allocation ControlWorkspace, control_step!, and run_realtime_loop! (mlockall, SCHEDFIFO, CPU pinning, absolute-time `clocknanosleep`, GC pause) plus a lock-free SPSC telemetry ring.\nVerification & codegen – allocation proofs via the AllocCheck extension (check_step_allocs) and standalone compilation via the StaticCompiler extension (compile_controller).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.ArtyHill"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.AbstractHILBackend","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractHILBackend\n\nSupertype of all hardware backends. A concrete backend B must implement\n\ninit_backend!(b::B)::Int32\nread_sensors!(y::AbstractVector, b::B)::Int32     # fill y in place\nwrite_actuators!(u::AbstractVector, b::B)::Int32  # consume u\nclose_backend!(b::B)::Int32\n\nread_sensors! and write_actuators! are called from the real-time thread and must not allocate on the Julia heap (verify with check_step_allocs). All functions return HIL_OK (0) on success and a negative code on failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.AbstractHILBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.AbstractPlant","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractPlant\n\nA plant model driven by SimulationBackend. Implement\n\nplant_output!(y, plant)   # write measurements of the current state into y\nplant_step!(plant, u)     # advance one sample period under input u\nreset_plant!(plant)       # return to the initial condition\n\nAll three must be allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.AbstractPlant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.CanFDFrame","page":"API reference","text":"Linux struct canfd_frame (72 bytes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.CanFDFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.CanFilter","page":"API reference","text":"Linux struct can_filter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.CanFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.CanFrame","page":"API reference","text":"Linux struct can_frame (16 bytes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.CanFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.CiA402Codec","page":"API reference","text":"CiA402Codec{N}(layout; mode=:csp, position_scale=1.0, velocity_scale=1.0,\n               torque_scale=1.0, auto_fault_reset=false)\n\nProcess-image codec for N CiA 402 axes, for use with EtherCATBackend(...; codec=CiA402Codec{N}(...)).\n\nCommands u[k]: target of axis k in user units — position (:csp), velocity (:csv) or torque (:cst). Raw value = u[k] * scale (e.g. position_scale = encoder counts per radian; torque in ‰ of rated torque).\nSensors y: [pos₁, vel₁, torque₁, pos₂, …] (3 per axis) in user units; unmapped objects read as 0.\nState machine: on every cycle the statusword is decoded and the controlword advances the drive towards Operation enabled — but only while cia402_enable!(codec, true) is set. Disabled, the drive is held in Ready to switch on (no torque). In :csp, the target follows the actual position until the axis is enabled, so enabling never causes a jump.\nFaults: a fault on any axis clears the enable flag for all axes, so after a reset nothing re-energizes until you call cia402_enable!(codec, true) again. Request a reset with cia402_fault_reset!, or pass auto_fault_reset=true to reset automatically (enable stays cleared either way). The reset is sent as rising edges on controlword bit 7 until the drive leaves Fault.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.CiA402Codec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.CiA402Layout","page":"API reference","text":"CiA402Layout(; out_stride, in_stride, controlword=0, target=2, mode_of_operation=-1,\n               statusword=0, position_actual=2, velocity_actual=6, torque_actual=-1)\n\nByte offsets of the CiA 402 objects inside one axis's slice of the output (RxPDO) and input (TxPDO) process image. Axis k starts at (k-1) * stride. Use -1 for an object that is not mapped. The defaults match a common minimal mapping: outputs 0x6040 controlword (u16), 0x607A/0x60FF/0x6071 target, inputs 0x6041 statusword (u16), 0x6064 position (i32), 0x606C velocity (i32). Check your drive's ESI / PDO assignment and adjust.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.CiA402Layout"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.CiA402Layout-Tuple{AbstractVector{PDOEntry}, AbstractVector{PDOEntry}}","page":"API reference","text":"CiA402Layout(rx_entries, tx_entries)\n\nDerive the layout from the PDO mapping you program with configure_pdo!, so the two cannot drift apart. rx_entries (outputs) may contain 0x6040 controlword, 0x607A/0x60FF/0x6071 target and 0x6060 mode; tx_entries (inputs) 0x6041 statusword, 0x6064 position, 0x606C velocity and 0x6077 torque. Other objects and padding just advance the offset. Strides are the PDO sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.CiA402Layout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.ControlWorkspace","page":"API reference","text":"ControlWorkspace{N,M,P,T}(; x0=zeros)\n\nPreallocated, fixed-size buffers for one control loop: x_hat (N estimated states), u_cmd (M commands), y_meas (P measurements). All buffers are MVectors, allocated once at construction and reused for every step, so their memory is contiguous and can be passed straight to C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.ControlWorkspace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.ControlWorkspace-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Integer, Integer}, Tuple{Integer, Integer, Integer}, Tuple{Integer, Integer, Integer, Type{T}}} where T","page":"API reference","text":"ControlWorkspace(N, M, P=N, T=Float64)\n\nConvenience constructor (not type-stable; call it once at setup time).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.ControlWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.DiscreteLinearPlant","page":"API reference","text":"DiscreteLinearPlant(A, B, C, x0)\n\nDiscrete-time state-space plant with N states, M inputs and P outputs, stored in StaticArrays so stepping it never allocates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.DiscreteLinearPlant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.ECError","page":"API reference","text":"Layout of SOEM's ec_errort (not packed); checked against the C headers in the EtherCAT tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.ECError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.EtherCATBackend","page":"API reference","text":"EtherCATBackend(iface; output_bytes, iomap_size=4096, codec=PackedFloat64Codec(),\n                preop_config=nothing, dc_cycle_ns=0, dc_shift_ns=0,\n                dc_frame_phase_ns=dc_cycle_ns ÷ 2, dc_align=dc_cycle_ns > 0)\n\nEtherCAT master using SOEM (LIBSOEM). output_bytes is the size of the output part of the process image (SOEM maps outputs first, then inputs). One control cycle is: read_sensors! receives the frame sent in the previous cycle, write_actuators! encodes commands and sends the next frame — the standard one-cycle pipeline. The expected working counter is taken from the last exchange during the transition to OPERATIONAL.\n\nDrive configuration (CoE). preop_config(backend, slave) is called for every slave while the network is in PRE-OP, before the process image is mapped. Use sdo_write! / configure_pdo! there to set parameters and PDO mappings; SOEM then reads the resulting mapping over CoE when it builds the process image. An exception aborts init_backend! (which then returns HIL_ERR_SYS).\n\nDistributed clocks. With dc_cycle_ns > 0, every slave's Sync0 is started with that cycle time (shifted by dc_shift_ns), which most drives require for cyclic synchronous modes. With dc_align (default on), run_realtime_loop! also steers its own wake-up times so that frames reach the reference clock at dc_frame_phase_ns within each DC cycle — use the same period_ns as dc_cycle_ns. See dc_phase_error_ns.\n\nRaw-socket access requires root or CAP_NET_RAW (sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep julia).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.EtherCATBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.Float64Codec","page":"API reference","text":"Float64Codec()\n\nDefault CAN payload codec: one native-endian Float64 per 8-byte slot. Define your own codec type with encode_can(codec, x)::NTuple{8,UInt8} and decode_can(codec, data::NTuple{8,UInt8}, dlc::UInt8) for real drive protocols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.Float64Codec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.LimitedController","page":"API reference","text":"LimitedController(controller!, umin, umax; max_rate=Inf, dt=1.0)\n\nWrap any controller with actuator protection, applied after it runs:\n\nnon-finite commands (NaN, ±Inf) are replaced by the previous command;\neach command is limited to change by at most max_rate * dt per step;\neach command is clamped to [umin[i], umax[i]].\n\numin, umax and max_rate may be scalars or length-M vectors. The previous command starts at zero clamped into the limits; call reset_limits! to change it. Allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.LimitedController"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.LoopStats","page":"API reference","text":"LoopStats\n\nTiming statistics filled by run_realtime_loop!. wake_late is the scheduling jitter (actual wake-up minus deadline), latency the compute time of control_step!, and an overrun is a step that finished after the next deadline. abort_reason is :none for a complete run, or :overruns, :errors or :exception when the loop stopped early. Use latency_percentile for percentiles from the preallocated histograms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.LoopStats"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.NonlinearPlant","page":"API reference","text":"NonlinearPlant(f, h, x0, Val(M))\n\nPlant defined by x⁺ = f(x, u) and y = h(x) where x, u and the returned values are SVectors. f and h must be type-stable and allocation-free. Useful for modelling gearbox backlash or torque ripple before touching hardware.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.NonlinearPlant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.PDOEntry","page":"API reference","text":"PDOEntry(index, subindex, bits)\n\nOne object mapped into a PDO, e.g. PDOEntry(0x6040, 0, 16) for the controlword. Use index = 0 for padding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.PDOEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.PackedFloat64Codec","page":"API reference","text":"PackedFloat64Codec()\n\nDefault process-image codec: outputs are packed Float64s starting at the output offset, inputs are packed Float64s starting at the input offset. Real drives (CiA 402 etc.) need a custom codec implementing\n\nencode_outputs!(iomap::Ptr{UInt8}, codec, u)\ndecode_inputs!(y, codec, iomap::Ptr{UInt8})\n\nwhere iomap already points at the start of the output / input region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.PackedFloat64Codec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.RTConfig","page":"API reference","text":"RTConfig(; period_ns, iterations, priority=80, cpu=-1, lock_memory=true,\n           disable_gc=true, strict=false, max_consecutive_overruns=0,\n           max_consecutive_errors=3, hist_bucket_ns=1_000, hist_buckets=2_000)\n\nReal-time loop settings.\n\npriority: SCHED_FIFO priority 1–99; 0 keeps the default scheduler.\ncpu: pin the loop thread to this CPU (isolate it with isolcpus=/nohz_full=); -1 = no pinning.\nlock_memory: mlockall(MCL_CURRENT | MCL_FUTURE) to prevent page faults.\ndisable_gc: run GC.gc() once, then pause the GC for the duration of the loop.\nstrict: throw if any of the above cannot be applied instead of continuing in degraded (soft) mode.\nmax_consecutive_overruns / max_consecutive_errors: watchdog. The loop stops (and drives the safe command, if one was given) after this many overruns / non-OK steps in a row. 0 disables the check.\nhist_bucket_ns, hist_buckets: resolution and range of the latency / jitter histograms used by latency_percentile. The last bucket collects everything above the range.\n\nSCHED_FIFO and mlockall need root or CAP_SYS_NICE / CAP_IPC_LOCK plus a suitable ulimit -r / ulimit -l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.RTConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.RealtimeLoop","page":"API reference","text":"RealtimeLoop\n\nHandle returned by spawn_realtime_loop. stop_loop!(h) asks the loop to finish its current cycle and exit; fetch(h) waits and returns the LoopStats (rethrowing any error from the loop); istaskdone(h) polls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.RealtimeLoop"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.SDOError","page":"API reference","text":"SDOError(slave, index, subindex, abort_code)\n\nA failed SDO transfer. abort_code is the CoE abort code from the slave, or 0 if the transfer failed without one (timeout, no mailbox).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.SDOError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.SPSCRing","page":"API reference","text":"SPSCRing{T}(capacity)\n\nLock-free single-producer / single-consumer ring buffer for isbits T. The real-time thread calls try_push! (never blocks, never allocates; returns false and counts a drop when full). A background thread calls try_pop! / drain! to move samples to disk or the network. capacity is rounded up to a power of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.SPSCRing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.SPSCRing-Tuple{ControlWorkspace, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"SPSCRing(ws::ControlWorkspace, capacity)\n\nTelemetry ring sized for the samples produced by ws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.SPSCRing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.SimulationBackend","page":"API reference","text":"SimulationBackend(plant)\n\nPure-software backend: sensors are read from plant, and every actuator write advances plant by one sample period. Use it for software-in-the-loop runs and CI before swapping in a hardware backend — the controller code is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.SimulationBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.SockaddrCan","page":"API reference","text":"Linux struct sockaddr_can (24 bytes on current kernels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.SockaddrCan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.SocketCANBackend","page":"API reference","text":"SocketCANBackend(iface; tx_base_id, rx_base_id, codec=Float64Codec(), extended=false,\n                 rx_count=0, canfd=false, heartbeat_id=0, heartbeat_every=100)\n\nRaw SocketCAN backend.\n\nClassic CAN (default): actuator u[i] is sent as one frame with id tx_base_id + i - 1; sensor y[i] is updated from any received frame with id rx_base_id + i - 1.\n\nCAN FD (canfd=true, interface must be FD-capable): all commands go out in one 64-byte frame with id tx_base_id (8-byte slot per value, so up to 8 values, updated atomically), and sensors are read from FD frames with id rx_base_id the same way.\n\nIn both modes the last value is held when no new frame arrived, and the socket is non-blocking: read_sensors! drains at most 4 * length(y) frames per cycle so its worst-case run time stays bounded.\n\nrx_count > 0 installs kernel receive filters for exactly the sensor ids, so unrelated bus traffic never reaches Julia (recommended on busy buses).\nheartbeat_id != 0 sends a 1-byte CANopen-style heartbeat (0x05, operational) with that id every heartbeat_every cycles from heartbeat!, for drives that cut power when the master goes silent.\n\nBring the interface up first, e.g. sudo ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 1000000 (add dbitrate 5000000 fd on for CAN FD) or, for testing, sudo ip link add dev vcan0 type vcan && sudo ip link set vcan0 mtu 72 up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.SocketCANBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.StaticFeedback","page":"API reference","text":"StaticFeedback(K, r=0)\n\nAllocation-free static output feedback u = -K (y - r) with K::SMatrix{M,P}. Also updates x_hat = y when N == P (a pass-through \"estimator\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.StaticFeedback"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArtyHill.TelemetrySample","page":"API reference","text":"TelemetrySample{P,M}\n\nOne logged control cycle: wake-up timestamp (CLOCK_MONOTONIC ns), wake-up lateness and compute latency (ns), step status, whether the step overran its deadline, measurements and commands.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.TelemetrySample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill._worker_threads-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Julia worker threads that can run tasks (excludes foreign/adopted thread slots).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill._worker_threads"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.can_filters-Tuple{SocketCANBackend}","page":"API reference","text":"Kernel filters matching exactly the sensor ids of b (empty when rx_count == 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.can_filters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.check_loop_allocs","page":"API reference","text":"check_loop_allocs(ws, controller!, backend; telemetry=nothing) -> Vector\n\nLike check_step_allocs but for the complete inner real-time loop (sleeping, timing, statistics and telemetry push). Requires using AllocCheck.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.check_loop_allocs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.check_step_allocs","page":"API reference","text":"check_step_allocs(ws, controller!, backend) -> Vector\n\nStatically analyse the LLVM IR of control_step! specialized for these argument types and return every possible heap-allocation / dynamic-dispatch site (empty vector = proven allocation-free). Requires using AllocCheck.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.check_step_allocs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.cia402_enable!-Tuple{CiA402Codec, Bool}","page":"API reference","text":"Allow (true) or forbid (false) the state machine to enable the drives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.cia402_enable!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.cia402_fault_reset!-Union{Tuple{CiA402Codec{N}}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{CiA402Codec{N}, Any}} where N","page":"API reference","text":"cia402_fault_reset!(codec, axis=:all)\n\nRequest a fault reset for one axis (or all). The codec then pulses controlword bit 7 until the drive leaves Fault. The drive ends up in Switch on disabled; re-enable explicitly with cia402_enable!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.cia402_fault_reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.cia402_state-Tuple{UInt16}","page":"API reference","text":"cia402_state(statusword) -> Symbol\ncia402_state(codec, axis) -> Symbol\n\nDecode a CiA 402 statusword into :not_ready, :switch_on_disabled, :ready_to_switch_on, :switched_on, :operation_enabled, :quick_stop, :fault_reaction or :fault.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.cia402_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.clean_telemetry","page":"API reference","text":"clean_telemetry(csv_path; outliers=\"hampel\", resample=nothing,\n                resample_method=\"linear\", columns=nothing) -> DataFrame\n\nPost-process a telemetry CSV written by write_telemetry_csv with KaosCleaner.jl: outliers in the measurement/command columns (y*, u*, or columns) become missing, and with resample=\"1ms\" the log is put on a regular time grid, ready for system identification. Requires Julia ≥ 1.12 and using KaosCleaner (Pkg.add(\"KaosCleaner\")).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.clean_telemetry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.compile_controller","page":"API reference","text":"compile_controller(f, argtypes, path, name; kind=:shlib) -> String\n\nCompile f(argtypes...) into a standalone, libjulia-free shared library (kind=:shlib) or executable (kind=:executable) in directory path using StaticCompiler.jl. f must take/return only C-compatible types (Ptr, numbers) and must not touch the Julia runtime (no GC allocation, no exceptions) — wrap raw pointers with StaticTools.MallocVector/unsafe_load. Requires using StaticCompiler, StaticTools.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.compile_controller"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.configure_pdo!-Tuple{EtherCATBackend, Integer, Integer, Integer, AbstractVector{PDOEntry}}","page":"API reference","text":"configure_pdo!(backend, slave, assign_index, pdo_index, entries)\n\nProgram a PDO mapping over CoE with the standard sequence: clear the assignment assign_index (0x1C12 outputs / 0x1C13 inputs), clear pdo_index (0x1600… / 0x1A00…), write the entries, set their count, then assign the PDO. Call it from preop_config. Returns the PDO size in bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.configure_pdo!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.control_step!-Union{Tuple{B}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{ControlWorkspace, F, B}} where {F, B<:AbstractHILBackend}","page":"API reference","text":"control_step!(ws, controller!, backend) -> Int32\n\nOne sense → compute → actuate cycle:\n\nread_sensors!(ws.y_meas, backend)\ncontroller!(ws.u_cmd, ws.x_hat, ws.y_meas)\nwrite_actuators!(ws.u_cmd, backend)\n\ncontroller! may be any callable (closure, functor). The method specializes on its concrete type, so with an allocation-free controller and backend the whole step allocates zero bytes — verify with check_step_allocs. Returns the first non-OK status, or HIL_OK.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.control_step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.cycle_correction_ns-Tuple{AbstractHILBackend, Int64}","page":"API reference","text":"cycle_correction_ns(backend, period_ns) -> Int64\n\nCalled by run_realtime_loop! once per cycle; the result is added to the next wake-up deadline. Backends with their own clock (EtherCAT distributed clocks) use it to phase-lock the loop to the bus. Default: 0. Must be allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.cycle_correction_ns"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.dc_phase_error_ns-Tuple{EtherCATBackend}","page":"API reference","text":"dc_phase_error_ns(backend) -> Int64\n\nLast measured deviation of the frame arrival phase (DC time modulo the DC cycle) from dc_frame_phase_ns, in ns, wrapped to (-cycle/2, cycle/2].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.dc_phase_error_ns"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.dc_time-Tuple{EtherCATBackend}","page":"API reference","text":"dc_time(backend) -> Int64\n\nSystem time (ns since 2000-01-01) of the DC reference clock, as captured by the most recent process-data exchange. 0 before the first exchange or without DC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.dc_time"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.default_rt_thread-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"Default thread for spawn_realtime_loop: the last thread of the :default pool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.default_rt_thread"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.drain!-Tuple{Any, SPSCRing}","page":"API reference","text":"drain!(f, r::SPSCRing) -> Int\n\nPop every currently available element and call f(x) on it. Returns the count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.drain!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.fixed_cstring-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{AbstractString, Val{N}}} where N","page":"API reference","text":"fixed_cstring(s, Val(N)) -> NTuple{N,UInt8}\n\nPack s into a NUL-terminated fixed-size buffer (for interface names etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.fixed_cstring"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.heartbeat!-Tuple{AbstractHILBackend, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"heartbeat!(backend, iteration) -> Int32\n\nCalled by run_realtime_loop! once per cycle after control_step!. Backends override it to emit a liveness signal for the drive's own watchdog (e.g. a CANopen heartbeat frame). Default: no-op returning HIL_OK. Must be allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.heartbeat!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.juliac_build","page":"API reference","text":"juliac_build(file; output, kind=:lib, trim=\"safe\", project=\"\", bundle=nothing,\n             ccallables=true, verbose=false) -> String\n\nBuild file with Julia 1.12's juliac (via JuliaC.jl) into a shared library (kind=:lib, exporting every Base.@ccallable function) or an executable (kind=:exe, file must define @main). Unlike compile_controller, the result links a trimmed libjulia, so it supports far more of the language (exceptions, most of Base) at the cost of a runtime dependency; pass bundle=\"dir\" to copy that runtime next to the output for deployment. Requires Julia ≥ 1.12 and using JuliaC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.juliac_build"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.latency_percentile-Tuple{LoopStats, Real}","page":"API reference","text":"latency_percentile(stats, p; kind=:latency) -> Int64\n\nUpper bound (ns) of the histogram bucket holding the p-quantile (0 < p ≤ 1) of step latency (kind=:latency) or wake-up jitter (kind=:wake). Returns typemax(Int64) if the quantile falls in the overflow bucket.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.latency_percentile"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.monotonic_ns-Tuple{}","page":"API reference","text":"monotonic_ns() -> UInt64\n\nCLOCK_MONOTONIC in nanoseconds (same clock clock_nanosleep sleeps on).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.monotonic_ns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.pid_bank","page":"API reference","text":"pid_bank(pids...; setpoints=zeros, inputs=1:M) -> controller\n\nTurn M DiscretePIDs.DiscretePIDs into one allocation-free ArtyHill controller: u[k] = calculate_control!(pids[k], setpoint[k], y[inputs[k]]). Change references at run time with bank.setpoint[k] = r. Requires using DiscretePIDs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.pid_bank"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.reset_limits!-Union{Tuple{LimitedController{C, M}}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{LimitedController{C, M}, Any}} where {C, M}","page":"API reference","text":"Set the limiter's notion of the previously applied command (e.g. the drive's current output).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.reset_limits!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.run_realtime_loop!-Tuple{ControlWorkspace, Any, AbstractHILBackend, RTConfig}","page":"API reference","text":"run_realtime_loop!(ws, controller!, backend, cfg::RTConfig;\n                   telemetry=nothing, safe_command=nothing, stop=nothing) -> LoopStats\nrun_realtime_loop!(ws, controller!, backend, period_ns, iterations; kwargs...)\n\nRun control_step!(ws, controller!, backend) every period_ns nanoseconds for iterations cycles on the calling OS thread:\n\nmlockall memory, pin the thread to cfg.cpu, switch it to SCHED_FIFO;\ncollect garbage once, then pause the GC;\nsleep with clock_nanosleep(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, TIMER_ABSTIME) against absolute deadlines, so there is no cumulative drift;\nafter each step call heartbeat!(backend, i), shift the next deadline by cycle_correction_ns (DC phase lock), and check the watchdog limits (cfg.max_consecutive_overruns, cfg.max_consecutive_errors);\non exit — normal, watchdog trip or exception — write safe_command (e.g. zero torque) if one was given, then restore scheduler, affinity, memory locking and GC state. Exceptions are rethrown after that.\n\ntelemetry may be an SPSCRing built with SPSCRing(ws, capacity); one TelemetrySample is pushed per cycle (dropped, never blocking, when full). stop may be a Threads.Atomic{Bool}: setting it ends the loop after the current cycle (abort_reason = :stopped, safe command still sent). To run the loop on its own thread, see spawn_realtime_loop.\n\nThe backend is not initialized or closed here — call init_backend! / close_backend! around it. Run a warm-up (e.g. a few control_step! calls) first so everything is compiled before the deadlines start.\n\nNote: with Julia's GC paused, other Julia threads must not allocate heavily during the loop. Run the loop on a dedicated thread (e.g. start Julia with --threads=2 and keep the consumer side light).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.run_realtime_loop!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.sdo_read-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{EtherCATBackend, Integer, Integer, Integer, Type{T}}} where T<:Union{Float32, Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"sdo_read(backend, slave, index, subindex, T; timeout_us=700_000) -> T\n\nRead object index:subindex of slave as type T over CoE. Throws an SDOError on failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.sdo_read"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.sdo_write!-Tuple{EtherCATBackend, Integer, Integer, Integer, Union{Float32, Integer}}","page":"API reference","text":"sdo_write!(backend, slave, index, subindex, value; timeout_us=700_000)\n\nWrite value (an Integer of the object's exact width, e.g. UInt16(…), or a Float32) to object index:subindex of slave (1-based) over CoE. Throws an SDOError with the slave's abort code on failure. Not real-time: use it in preop_config or between runs, not inside the control loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.sdo_write!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.send_safe_command!-Tuple{ControlWorkspace, AbstractHILBackend, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"send_safe_command!(ws, backend, safe_command) -> Int32\n\nCopy safe_command into ws.u_cmd and write it to the backend, bypassing the controller. Used by run_realtime_loop! on watchdog trips, exceptions and normal exit; call it yourself from emergency-stop handlers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.send_safe_command!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.set_libsoem!-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"set_libsoem!(path)\n\nPoint ArtyHill at a specific SOEM shared library (absolute path or soname). Saved in LocalPreferences.toml of the active project; restart Julia to apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.set_libsoem!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.spawn_realtime_loop-Tuple{ControlWorkspace, Any, AbstractHILBackend, RTConfig}","page":"API reference","text":"spawn_realtime_loop(ws, controller!, backend, cfg::RTConfig;\n                    thread=default_rt_thread(), telemetry=nothing, safe_command=nothing) -> RealtimeLoop\nspawn_realtime_loop(ws, controller!, backend, period_ns, iterations; thread, kwargs...)\n\nRun run_realtime_loop! on Julia thread thread (default: the last thread of the :default pool), leaving the calling thread free for telemetry, UI or a REPL. The task is pinned to that thread and never yields while the loop runs, so the SCHED_FIFO/CPU-affinity settings apply to exactly that OS thread. Start Julia with at least two threads (julia --threads=2). Use iterations=typemax(Int64) with stop_loop! for an open-ended run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.spawn_realtime_loop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.stop_loop!-Tuple{RealtimeLoop}","page":"API reference","text":"Ask a spawned real-time loop to stop after its current cycle. Returns the handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.stop_loop!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.telemetry_type-Union{Tuple{ControlWorkspace{N, M, P}}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{N}} where {N, M, P}","page":"API reference","text":"Element type of the telemetry ring matching workspace ws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.telemetry_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.try_pop!-Union{Tuple{SPSCRing{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"API reference","text":"Consumer side. Returns the oldest element, or nothing if empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.try_pop!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.try_push!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SPSCRing{T}, T}} where T","page":"API reference","text":"Producer side. Returns true if x was stored, false (and counts a drop) if full.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.try_push!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArtyHill.verijul_backend","page":"API reference","text":"verijul_backend(dut; inputs, outputs, clock=:clk, cycles_per_step=1,\n                input_scale=1.0, output_scale=1.0, signed_outputs=true) -> AbstractHILBackend\n\nCo-simulate RTL as the \"hardware\": a VeriJul.jl DUT (Verilator model) becomes a backend. Each write_actuators! converts u[k] * input_scale[k] to an integer on input port inputs[k] and clocks the design cycles_per_step times; read_sensors! reads output port outputs[k] (two's complement when signed_outputs) divided by output_scale[k]. Ports must be ≤ 64 bits. Use it to test FPGA logic (PWM generators, encoder decoders, current loops) against the Julia controller before synthesis. Requires using VeriJul.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.verijul_backend"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ArtyHill.write_telemetry_csv-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{IO, AbstractArray{TelemetrySample{P, M}, 1}}} where {P, M}","page":"API reference","text":"write_telemetry_csv(io, samples)\n\nDump collected TelemetrySamples as CSV (header + one row per cycle) for post-processing / system identification outside the real-time process.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.write_telemetry_csv"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#ArtyHill.LIBSOEM","page":"API reference","text":"LIBSOEM\n\nLibrary used for SOEM calls (default \"libsoem\", found via the system loader path). It is a compile-time constant so hot-path ccalls bind statically (no indirect function-pointer call, which also lets AllocCheck prove the step allocation-free). Change it with set_libsoem!, which stores a Preferences.jl setting and takes effect after restarting Julia.\n\nBuild SOEM 1.4 as a shared library: cmake -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON .. && make && sudo make install.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArtyHill.LIBSOEM"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#Tutorial:-bring-up-a-real-servo-drive","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"This tutorial takes a CiA 402 servo drive on EtherCAT from \"cable plugged in\" to \"moving under ArtyHill's real-time loop\", one stage at a time. It uses bench/drive_bench.jl, which stops and asks before it enables the drive and again before it moves it.\n\nwarning: The motor will move\nBefore you start: clamp the motor to the bench with no load coupled, wire the drive's STO (Safe Torque Off) input to a hardware E-stop within reach, and keep the defaults (an 18° move limited to 0.5 rev/s) for the first run. Software limits are not a substitute for a hardware interlock.","title":"Tutorial: bring up a real servo drive"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#What-you-need","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"A native Linux machine. WSL2, containers on macOS and most VMs cannot pass raw Ethernet frames reliably. A small x86 PC with an Intel i210/i211 NIC is ideal; a Raspberry Pi 5 works too.\nA dedicated Ethernet port for EtherCAT, cabled directly to the drive's IN port (no switch).\nA CiA 402 drive that supports cyclic synchronous position mode (CSP) and distributed clocks, already tuned with the vendor's tool so that it holds position stably on its own.\nThe drive's encoder resolution (counts per motor revolution), from its manual or object 0x608F.\nJulia ≥ 1.10, a C compiler, curl, and sudo rights.","title":"What you need"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#1.-Prepare-the-network-port","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"Find the port's name with ip link. Here it is eth1. Stop NetworkManager from managing it (it would try DHCP and send frames of its own), bring it up, and switch off interrupt coalescing so frames are not held back:\n\nsudo nmcli device set eth1 managed no\nsudo ip link set eth1 up\nsudo ethtool -C eth1 rx-usecs 0","title":"1. Prepare the network port"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#2.-Build-SOEM-and-point-ArtyHill-at-it","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"ArtyHill talks to the drives through SOEM 1.4 as a shared library. The emulator test script downloads and builds it for you:\n\ntest/ethercat/run.sh      # builds ~/.cache/artyhill-soem/libsoem.so, then runs the emulator tests\n\nThen set the library for the bench environment, which is saved as a preference (set_libsoem!):\n\njulia --project=bench -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=bench -e 'using ArtyHill; set_libsoem!(expanduser(\"~/.cache/artyhill-soem/libsoem.so\"))'","title":"2. Build SOEM and point ArtyHill at it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#3.-Get-the-privileges","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"Raw EtherCAT frames need CAP_NET_RAW; the real-time loop needs CAP_SYS_NICE (for SCHED_FIFO) and CAP_IPC_LOCK (for mlockall). bench/rtrun.sh runs any command as you with exactly those three capabilities, through a short-lived systemd unit:\n\nbench/rtrun.sh julia --version\n\nPrefer it over the alternatives: setcap on the julia binary puts Julia in secure-execution mode, where the loader may no longer find libjulia, and sudo julia uses root's package depot and leaves root-owned files behind.","title":"3. Get the privileges"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#4.-Run-the-bring-up","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"COUNTS_PER_REV=131072 bench/rtrun.sh julia --threads=2 --project=bench bench/drive_bench.jl eth1\n\nThe script walks through five stages. Here is what each one does, and what to check before you answer \"y\" to the next.\n\nStage 1: scan, configure, operational. SOEM scans the line. In PRE-OP, for every drive, the script reads its identity (0x1018) and supported modes (0x6502), sets CSP mode (0x6060 = 8) over CoE (sdo_write!) and programs a minimal PDO mapping (configure_pdo!):\n\nDirection Objects\noutputs (RxPDO 0x1600) controlword 0x6040, target position 0x607A\ninputs (TxPDO 0x1A00) statusword 0x6041, position 0x6064, velocity 0x606C\n\nThen DC Sync0 is started at the loop period and the line goes to OP.\n\n  slave 1: vendor 0x..., product 0x..., supported modes 0x...\n  OP reached: 1 slave(s), expected working counter 3\n\nIf this fails, see Troubleshooting below.\n\nStage 2: read positions, no torque. For one second the drive stays in Ready to switch on while positions are read. Turn the shaft by hand by a known amount (if that is safe) and run again: the reported position should change by that fraction of a revolution, with the sign you expect. If it is off by a factor, COUNTS_PER_REV is wrong.\n\nStage 3: enable and hold. After you confirm, cia402_enable! lets the CiA 402 state machine walk the drive to Operation enabled. In CSP, the target is the actual position until the moment of enabling, so the motor should energize without any jump. A clunk here means the drive is interpreting the target differently (units, or a position offset): stop and check before going further.\n\nStage 4: move. After you confirm, the real-time loop runs at 1 kHz (SCHED_FIFO priority 80, memory locked, GC paused) and commands one smooth out-and-back move, MOVE_REV revolutions over MOVE_SECONDS, starting and ending at zero speed. A LimitedController caps the commanded speed at MAX_RPS whatever the profile says. The watchdog stops the loop after 3 consecutive communication errors or 5 consecutive overruns.\n\nStage 5: disable and close. Always runs, even after an error in stages 2–4: the drive is held, disabled (no torque) and the line closed.","title":"4. Run the bring-up"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#5.-Read-the-results","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"LoopStats: 4500 iterations, 0 overruns, 0 errors\n  wake-up jitter mean ..., p99 ≤ ..., p99.9 ≤ ..., max ...\n  step latency:   mean ..., p99 ≤ ..., p99.9 ≤ ..., max ...\n  mlockall=true SCHED_FIFO=true pinned=false gc_off=true safe_cmd_sent=false\n  abort reason: none, DC phase error now: ... ns\n  max following error: ...° (... counts) over 4500 cycles\n  telemetry → bench/drive_telemetry.csv\n\nmlockall=true SCHED_FIFO=true: the privileges worked. If either is false, the run was in soft real-time mode and the timing numbers mean little.\nOverruns and errors should both be 0. Errors are cycles where the working counter was short, meaning a frame was lost or a drive did not answer.\nWake-up jitter is how late the loop woke up. On a stock kernel, expect occasional spikes of hundreds of µs; on PREEMPTRT with an isolated CPU, a few tens of µs at worst (see [Tutorial: PREEMPTRT latency](@ref)).\nDC phase error is how far frames land from the target point in the Sync0 cycle (dc_phase_error_ns). It should settle well below the period; a value that keeps growing means the loop is not locked.\nFollowing error is the largest gap between commanded and actual position. It reflects the drive's own tuning as much as ArtyHill.\ndrive_telemetry.csv has one row per cycle: timestamps, jitter, latency, status, the three readings per axis (y1…: position, velocity, torque in radians, rad/s and raw units) and the commands (u1…). Plot u1 against y1 to see the tracking.","title":"5. Read the results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#6.-Next-steps","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"Raise MOVE_REV, then MAX_RPS, a little at a time, and couple the load only after several clean runs. Pin the loop to an isolated CPU with CPU=3 once the PREEMPT_RT setup is in place. To run your own controller, copy the script and replace OutAndBack with any callable (u, x, y) -> nothing: y holds [pos₁, vel₁, torque₁, pos₂, …] and u the target per axis.","title":"6. Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#Settings","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"All are environment variables, so pass them before bench/rtrun.sh.\n\nVariable Default Meaning\nAXES 1 drives on the line; all get the same move\nCOUNTS_PER_REV 131072 encoder counts per revolution\nMOVE_REV 0.05 move amplitude, revolutions\nMOVE_SECONDS 4 duration of the out-and-back move\nMAX_RPS 0.5 hard limit on commanded speed, rev/s\nCYCLE_US 1000 loop and DC Sync0 period, µs\nCPU -1 pin the loop to this CPU (-1: no pinning)\nCONFIGURE_PDO 1 program the PDO mapping; 0 for fixed-PDO drives","title":"Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/drive_bringup/#drive-troubleshooting","page":"Bring up a real servo drive","text":"init_backend! fails, or 0 slaves found. Check the interface name, that the link LED is on, that the cable goes into the drive's IN port, and that nothing else (NetworkManager, a second master) uses the port. Run with bench/rtrun.sh, since raw sockets need CAP_NET_RAW.\n\nSDOError with an abort code in stage 1. The message names the object and the reason. 0x06020000 (object does not exist) on 0x6502 is harmless. On 0x1C12/0x1600 it usually means the drive has a fixed PDO mapping: look up the mapping in the drive's ESI file or manual, edit RX_MAP and TX_MAP at the top of the script to list exactly those objects in that order, and run with CONFIGURE_PDO=0. The codec layout is derived from the same lists, so it always matches.\n\nThe drive never reaches OP. Many drives refuse OP in CSP without DC Sync0 at a period they support; try CYCLE_US=2000 or 4000. Check the drive's error display or its error code object (0x603F).\n\nNot operation_enabled in stage 3. The drive is in Fault or STO is active. Release the E-stop, clear the cause shown by the drive, and rerun. From your own code, cia402_fault_reset! followed by cia402_enable! does the same.\n\nErrors during the move. A lost frame now and then points to the NIC or interrupt coalescing (step 1); bursts of overruns point to the scheduler (run the latency tutorial first).","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#Tutorial:-PREEMPT_RT-latency","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"How late does ArtyHill's loop wake up, in the worst case, on a properly set up real-time Linux machine? This tutorial sets one up and measures it next to cyclictest, the standard kernel latency benchmark, under the same settings and the same background load. The difference between the two is what Julia and ArtyHill add on top of the kernel.","title":"Tutorial: PREEMPT_RT latency"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#What-you-need","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"A native Linux machine you can reboot and change the kernel of. WSL2 and most VMs run the host's scheduler underneath and give meaningless numbers.\nAt least 4 CPU cores: one is set aside for the loop, the rest carry the system and the background load. The examples use CPU 3; pick the last core of your machine.\nJulia ≥ 1.10 and sudo rights.","title":"What you need"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#1.-Install-a-PREEMPT_RT-kernel","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"PREEMPT_RT makes almost all of the kernel preemptible, so a high-priority thread runs within microseconds of becoming ready. Since Linux 6.12 it is part of the mainline kernel; distributions ship it as a separate package:\n\nDebian 13: sudo apt install linux-image-rt-amd64 (or -rt-arm64).\nUbuntu 24.04 LTS: sudo pro attach (Ubuntu Pro is free for personal use), then sudo pro enable realtime-kernel.\nRaspberry Pi 5 and others: build a kernel ≥ 6.12 with CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT=y.\n\nReboot into it and check:\n\ncat /sys/kernel/realtime    # 1\nuname -v                    # contains PREEMPT_RT","title":"1. Install a PREEMPT_RT kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#2.-Set-aside-a-CPU","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"Add these to the kernel command line. On GRUB systems, append them to GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT in /etc/default/grub, then run sudo update-grub and reboot:\n\nisolcpus=3 nohz_full=3 rcu_nocbs=3 irqaffinity=0-2\n\nParameter Effect on CPU 3\nisolcpus=3 the scheduler puts no ordinary tasks there\nnohz_full=3 no periodic timer tick while a single task runs\nrcu_nocbs=3 kernel RCU callbacks run on the other CPUs\nirqaffinity=0-2 device interrupts go to the other CPUs\n\nCheck with cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/isolated (prints 3). Disable irqbalance, which would otherwise move interrupts back:\n\nsudo systemctl disable --now irqbalance","title":"2. Set aside a CPU"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#3.-Remove-sources-of-jitter","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"In the BIOS/UEFI: disable deep C-states (allow C1 at most), Turbo Boost and SMT/Hyper-Threading if you can. Waking a core from a deep sleep state alone can cost 100 µs.\nFix the CPU frequency:\nsudo apt install linux-cpupower    # Debian; linux-tools-common on Ubuntu\nsudo cpupower frequency-set -g performance","title":"3. Remove sources of jitter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#4.-Install-the-tools","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"sudo apt install rt-tests stress-ng\njulia --project=bench -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nrt-tests provides cyclictest; stress-ng generates background load.","title":"4. Install the tools"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#5.-Measure","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"Start ten minutes of load on the other CPUs: CPU work, disk I/O and memory pressure, which is what exposes latency problems.\n\ntaskset -c 0-2 stress-ng --cpu 3 --io 2 --vm 1 --vm-bytes 25% --timeout 15m &\n\nThen run both benchmarks, one after the other, at 1 kHz for 600 000 cycles (10 minutes each), on CPU 3, at SCHED_FIFO priority 80. bench/rtrun.sh gives them the privileges they need, as your own user (see Tutorial: bring up a real servo drive):\n\nbench/rtrun.sh ./bench/compare_cyclictest.sh 1000 600000 3 80\n\nThe script prints the kernel it runs on, then each result:\n\nkernel: ...-rt-amd64   preempt: 1\nsettings: period=1000us iterations=600000 cpu=3 prio=80\n\n=== cyclictest ===\n# Min Latencies: ...\n# Avg Latencies: ...\n# Max Latencies: ...\n# p99 <= ... us, p99.9 <= ... us\n\n=== ArtyHill (wake-up jitter is the comparable number) ===\nLoopStats: 600000 iterations, 0 overruns, 0 errors\n  wake-up jitter mean ..., p99 ≤ ..., p99.9 ≤ ..., max ...\n  step latency:   mean ..., p99 ≤ ..., p99.9 ≤ ..., max ...\n  mlockall=true SCHED_FIFO=true pinned=true gc_off=true safe_cmd_sent=false\n\nFull histograms, in 1 µs buckets, are written to bench/cyclictest_hist.txt and bench/artyhill_hist.csv.","title":"5. Measure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#6.-Read-the-results","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"Check the last line first: mlockall, SCHED_FIFO and pinned must all be true. Otherwise the loop ran in soft real-time mode and the numbers do not count.\nCompare ArtyHill's wake-up jitter with cyclictest's latency. Both measure the same thing: how long after its deadline the thread actually woke up. Whatever ArtyHill shows beyond cyclictest is the cost of Julia and the loop.\nThe maximum is what matters for a control loop, not the mean: a 1 kHz loop must finish every cycle within 1 ms, including the worst one. p99.9 tells you whether the maximum is a single outlier or a pattern.\nStep latency is the compute time of one control_step! (here with a simulated plant). Wake-up jitter plus step latency must stay below the period, with margin for the backend's I/O.\nOverruns count cycles that finished after the next deadline. On a well-set-up machine, there should be none.\n\nFor contrast, repeat the measurement without PREEMPT_RT (boot the stock kernel) and without isolation (pass CPU -1): that shows how much each step contributes.","title":"6. Read the results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#7.-Record-the-numbers","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"Write down, for each configuration:\n\nKernel Isolation Load cyclictest max / p99.9 (µs) ArtyHill wake max / p99.9 (µs) ArtyHill step max (µs) Overruns\nPREEMPT_RT CPU 3 stress-ng    \nstock CPU 3 stress-ng    \nstock none stress-ng    \n\nalong with the CPU model, the kernel version (uname -r) and the BIOS settings you changed. Latency numbers are only comparable between identical setups.","title":"7. Record the numbers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/preempt_rt/#Troubleshooting","page":"PREEMPT_RT latency","text":"mlockall or SCHED_FIFO is false. Run through bench/rtrun.sh. If you use plain sudo instead, Julia runs as root with root's package depot.\n\ncyclictest not found. Install rt-tests (step 4).\n\nHuge spikes (milliseconds) every few seconds. Usually firmware: SMIs (System Management Interrupts) from the BIOS, which no kernel setting can stop. Try disabling power management and hardware monitoring options in the BIOS; on some machines the spikes cannot be removed.\n\nArtyHill is much worse than cyclictest. Check that nothing else in the Julia process allocates heavily while the loop runs: the GC is paused for the whole process, but other threads can still trigger page faults. Run the loop alone with --threads=2, as the benchmark does.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Example:-cycloidal-drive,-from-geometry-to-controller","page":"Example: cycloidal drive","text":"examples/cycloidal_drive.jl walks through the whole prototyping loop for a robot joint with a cycloidal reducer.\n\nGeometry → model. The reduction ratio and the output backlash come from the reducer design: CycloidCore.backlash(DriveParams()) from cycloidal-generator when it is installed (9:1, 82.5 arcmin for the default Ø80 mm design), otherwise from the same precomputed numbers.\nSimulation. Motor → backlash + compliance → load with viscous and Coulomb friction, as a NonlinearPlant, run through run_realtime_loop! with an SPSCRing recording telemetry.\nIdentification from telemetry.\nBacklash from the hysteresis between the motor and load encoders under a slow triangle motion: 84.8 arcmin identified vs 82.5 from the geometry. The difference is the elastic wind-up of the transmission.\nInertias, damping and friction by least squares on the energy balance of the two masses, N·u = Jm·N²·φ̈m + bm·N²·φ̇m + JL·θ̈l + bL·θ̇l + Fc·sgn(θ̇l). Adding the motor and load equations cancels the gear torque, so this holds inside and outside the backlash. All five parameters come back within 2 %.\nController sweep. For ωn = 25, 40 and 60 rad/s, PD gains are computed from the identified model, and two feedback structures follow the same 1 s minimum-jerk move:\nωn [rad/s] load-side PD (RMS / p-p, mrad) motor-side PD + backlash compensation\n25 1.64 / 1.73 1.35 / 1.75\n40 0.09 / 0.19 0.27 / 0.20\n60 3.28 / 10.45 (hunting) 0.34 / 0.01\nLoad-side feedback puts the backlash inside the loop. It can be excellent, as at ωn = 40, but it hunts at higher bandwidth. Motor-side feedback keeps the backlash outside the loop and stays quiet across the sweep, at the cost of a small static offset that only the load encoder could remove. The example reports these numbers rather than declaring a winner: the right structure depends on your bandwidth and accuracy targets.\n\nRun it with:\n\njulia --threads=2 --project=examples examples/cycloidal_drive.jl\n\nThe test suite runs the same script and checks the identification accuracy.","title":"Example: cycloidal drive, from geometry to controller"},{"category":"section","location":"codegen/#Code-generation","page":"Code generation","text":"Two routes produce a library that a C/C++ real-time target can call.\n\n compile_controller (StaticCompiler.jl) juliac_build (JuliaC.jl)\nJulia 1.10 / 1.11 ≥ 1.12\nRuntime none: depends on libc only trimmed libjulia (bundle it with bundle=)\nLanguage subset no GC allocation, no exceptions, raw pointers most of Julia, including ArtyHill's own types","title":"Code generation"},{"category":"section","location":"codegen/#StaticCompiler","page":"Code generation","text":"using ArtyHill, StaticCompiler, StaticTools\npd(u::Ptr{Float64}, x::Ptr{Float64}, y::Ptr{Float64}) =\n    (unsafe_store!(u, -40unsafe_load(y, 1) - 12unsafe_load(y, 2)); Int32(0))\ncompile_controller(pd, (Ptr{Float64}, Ptr{Float64}, Ptr{Float64}), \"build\", \"pd\")","title":"StaticCompiler"},{"category":"section","location":"codegen/#juliac","page":"Code generation","text":"Write a file with Base.@ccallable entry points (see examples/juliac_controller.jl, which wraps a LimitedController), then:\n\nusing ArtyHill, JuliaC\njuliac_build(\"examples/juliac_controller.jl\"; output=\"build/libpd_limited\")","title":"juliac"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Backends","page":"Backends","text":"A backend implements four functions, each returning HIL_OK (0) or a negative HIL_ERR_* code. Exceptions allocate, so keep them out of the hot path:\n\nstruct MyBackend <: AbstractHILBackend ... end\nArtyHill.init_backend!(b::MyBackend)\nArtyHill.read_sensors!(y, b::MyBackend)      # fill y in place, no allocations\nArtyHill.write_actuators!(u, b::MyBackend)   # no allocations\nArtyHill.close_backend!(b::MyBackend)\nArtyHill.heartbeat!(b::MyBackend, i)         # optional, called once per cycle\n\nVerify the hot path with check_step_allocs(ws, controller, backend) (needs using AllocCheck).","title":"Backends"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Simulation","page":"Backends","text":"SimulationBackend wraps an AbstractPlant: DiscreteLinearPlant for LTI models, or NonlinearPlant from your own f(x, u) / h(x), which is handy for gearbox backlash or torque ripple.","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#SocketCAN","page":"Backends","text":"SocketCANBackend talks to Linux raw CAN sockets directly through libc.\n\nClassic CAN: one frame per value, consecutive ids from tx_base_id / rx_base_id.\nCAN FD (canfd=true): all commands in one 64-byte frame, updated atomically.\nrx_count installs kernel receive filters; heartbeat_id emits a liveness frame.\nPayloads go through a codec (Float64Codec by default). Implement encode_can / decode_can for your drive's protocol.","title":"SocketCAN"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#EtherCAT","page":"Backends","text":"EtherCATBackend drives SOEM 1.4's ec_* API. Point it at your SOEM build with set_libsoem!. The default codec packs Float64s. CiA402Codec speaks the CiA 402 servo profile: it decodes the statusword, steps the drive's state machine towards Operation enabled (only after cia402_enable!), scales targets and feedback, and in CSP mode keeps the target on the actual position until the drive is enabled.\n\nA fault on any axis latches the enable flag off. Clear it with cia402_fault_reset!, which pulses controlword bit 7 until the drive leaves Fault, then re-enable explicitly. Nothing re-energizes on its own.","title":"EtherCAT"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Configuring-drives-over-CoE","page":"Backends","text":"Most servo drives expect their PDO mapping and parameters to be written over the mailbox (SDO) in PRE-OP. Pass a preop_config(backend, slave) function; it runs for every slave before the process image is mapped, and SOEM then reads the mapping you programmed:\n\nrx = [PDOEntry(0x6040, 0, 16), PDOEntry(0x607A, 0, 32)]                 # controlword, target position\ntx = [PDOEntry(0x6041, 0, 16), PDOEntry(0x6064, 0, 32), PDOEntry(0x606C, 0, 32)]\n\nfunction preop!(b, slave)\n    sdo_write!(b, slave, 0x6060, 0, Int8(8))            # cyclic synchronous position\n    sdo_write!(b, slave, 0x6065, 0, UInt32(300_000))    # following-error window\n    configure_pdo!(b, slave, 0x1C12, 0x1600, rx)\n    configure_pdo!(b, slave, 0x1C13, 0x1A00, tx)\nend\n\nlayout = CiA402Layout(rx, tx)                           # offsets derived from the same mapping\ncodec = CiA402Codec{2}(layout; position_scale=10_000 / 2π)\nb = EtherCATBackend(\"eth0\"; output_bytes=2layout.out_stride, codec, preop_config=preop!,\n                    dc_cycle_ns=1_000_000)\n\nsdo_read / sdo_write! throw an SDOError carrying the drive's CoE abort code and its meaning (for example \"attempt to write a read-only object\"). They work in OP too, but not inside the control loop.","title":"Configuring drives over CoE"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Distributed-clocks","page":"Backends","text":"With dc_cycle_ns > 0, init_backend! starts Sync0 on every slave, which most drives require before they accept OP in a cyclic synchronous mode. With dc_align (default on), run_realtime_loop! phase-locks its wake-ups to the DC reference clock through cycle_correction_ns, using a PI law on the frame phase (the same as SOEM's ec_sync example). Frames then reach the drives at dc_frame_phase_ns within each cycle. Use the same period_ns as dc_cycle_ns, and watch dc_phase_error_ns and dc_time.","title":"Distributed clocks"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Testing-without-hardware","page":"Backends","text":"test/ethercat/run.sh builds SOEM 1.4 and runs the unmodified master against test/ethercat/ecat_emu.c, a frame-level emulator of a line of CiA 402 drives, over a veth pair in an unprivileged network namespace. It covers:\n\nthe slave scan (SII EEPROM) and FMMU/SM configuration;\nCoE SDO reads, writes and aborts, and PDO remapping;\nDC Sync0, including drives that refuse OP without it, and phase locking;\nthe transition to OP, CSP motion, faults, and a simulated cable pull.","title":"Testing without hardware"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Integration-tests-on-a-virtual-bus","page":"Backends","text":"sudo modprobe vcan\nsudo ip link add dev vcan0 type vcan && sudo ip link set vcan0 mtu 72 up\nARTYHILL_VCAN=vcan0 julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Integration tests on a virtual bus"},{"category":"section","location":"#ArtyHill.jl","page":"Home","text":"Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) and real-time embedded prototyping in Julia. One controller, unchanged, runs against a pure simulation, against SocketCAN / EtherCAT hardware, against RTL in a Verilator co-simulation, and finally as a standalone C-callable library.\n\nLayer Contents\nHardware abstraction SimulationBackend, SocketCANBackend, EtherCATBackend with CiA402Codec, verijul_backend\nDeterministic runtime ControlWorkspace, control_step!, run_realtime_loop!, SPSCRing\nSafety LimitedController, watchdog limits in RTConfig, send_safe_command!, heartbeat!\nVerification & codegen check_step_allocs, check_loop_allocs, compile_controller, juliac_build","title":"ArtyHill.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using ArtyHill, AllocCheck\n\nconst DT = 1e-3\nplant   = DiscreteLinearPlant([1 DT; 0 1], reshape([DT^2/2, DT], 2, 1), [1.0 0; 0 1], [1.0, 0])\nbackend = SimulationBackend(plant)\nws      = ControlWorkspace{2,1,2}()                       # N states, M commands, P measurements\nctrl    = LimitedController(StaticFeedback([40.0 12.0]), -5.0, 5.0; max_rate=1e3, dt=DT)\n\n@assert isempty(check_loop_allocs(ws, ctrl, backend))     # static proof: zero heap allocations\n\nring = SPSCRing(ws, 1 << 14)\ninit_backend!(backend)\nstats = run_realtime_loop!(ws, ctrl, backend, 1_000_000, 10_000;\n                           telemetry=ring, priority=80, cpu=3, safe_command=[0.0])\nclose_backend!(backend)\n\nSwap SimulationBackend(plant) for SocketCANBackend(\"can0\"; ...) or EtherCATBackend(\"eth0\"; ...) and nothing else changes.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"extensions/#Ecosystem-extensions","page":"Ecosystem extensions","text":"Each extension loads automatically when its package is loaded alongside ArtyHill.\n\nLoad You get\nusing AllocCheck check_step_allocs, check_loop_allocs\nusing StaticCompiler, StaticTools compile_controller\nusing JuliaC (Julia ≥ 1.12) juliac_build\nusing DiscretePIDs pid_bank: DiscretePIDs.jl controllers as one allocation-free ArtyHill controller\nusing VeriJul verijul_backend: co-simulate Verilog/RTL as the plant or I/O hardware\nusing KaosCleaner (Julia ≥ 1.12) clean_telemetry: outlier removal and resampling of telemetry logs","title":"Ecosystem extensions"},{"category":"section","location":"extensions/#RTL-co-simulation-with-VeriJul","page":"Ecosystem extensions","text":"using ArtyHill, VeriJul\ndut = load_dut(\"pwm_current_loop.v\", \"pwm_current_loop\")\nbackend = verijul_backend(dut; inputs=[:duty], outputs=[:current_adc],\n                          cycles_per_step=1000, input_scale=4095, output_scale=100)\n\nThe FPGA logic is clocked cycles_per_step times per control period, so the Julia controller sees the same sample-to-actuation timing it will see on hardware.","title":"RTL co-simulation with VeriJul"},{"category":"section","location":"extensions/#Cleaning-telemetry","page":"Ecosystem extensions","text":"KaosCleaner is a registered package (Julia ≥ 1.12): Pkg.add(\"KaosCleaner\").\n\nusing ArtyHill, KaosCleaner\ndf = clean_telemetry(\"telemetry.csv\"; outliers=\"hampel\", resample=\"1ms\")","title":"Cleaning telemetry"}]
}
